 | Enable Diffraction Streaks | bool | true | Grow sunstars out of very bright sources the way a real iris diffracts light off its blade edges: invisible wide open, appearing around f/8, sharp by f/16. |
 | Blade Rotation (deg) | 0-360 | 0 | Phase of the iris, rotating the whole set of rays. Rays are spread evenly, so the pattern repeats every 360 / ray count degrees. This same rotation also turns the bokeh polygon — one iris, one orientation. |
 | Streak Model | Analytic / Wave | **Wave** | Which model computes the sunstar. **Wave optics** transforms the real pupil — blade count, blade curvature, shape mask, spherical aberration — into its diffraction pattern, so 9 blades give 18 rays and stopping down lengthens them by itself. **Analytic** paints the older line pairs with a hand-tuned f-number ramp; keep it for an A/B or when the wave kernel is switched off. |
-| Streak Amount | 0-4 (slider 0-2) | 1.0 | Strength of the wave glare. 1 is the physical answer: the source deposits exactly the fraction of its light the pupil diffracts away, typically 5-15 % (about 7 % for an 8-blade iris). Above 1 is the exaggeration side. **Wave only**: in Analytic the f-number ramp still owns the amount. |
+| Streak Amount | 0-200 (slider 0-100) | **20** | How much brighter the real source is than the clamped HDR value standing in for it — a **source radiance boost**, not a style knob. 1 takes the HDR value as the true radiance: physically exact, and nearly invisible, because the rays carry only ~0.5 % of the light spread over hundreds of pixels. 10–100 reproduces the sunstar a real lamp or the sun of that kind draws; the pattern's shape and falloff stay physical at every value. **Wave only**: in Analytic the f-number ramp still owns the amount. |
 | Streak Reach | 0-1 | 0.35 | How far the rays are drawn, as a fraction of frame height. This is where the faint far-field tail is **cut off**, not how bright it is: a cost and coverage choice, not a property of the lens. **Wave only.** |
 | Streak Dispersion | 0-1 | 1.0 | Spread of the rays' rainbow. Every wavelength diffracts to a different size, which is why a real ray runs blue near the source and red at the tip. 0 = one white pattern at 550 nm. **Wave only.** |
 
